#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/id #uuid "90dc8e4f-e341-5487-9896-ab34efdf5847" :question/math-academy-id "e-20875" :question/problem "Plot the curve of $y=8^{x+2}-4$." :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y=8^x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6351/Source/Images/5db20c0c2a5b286b81d3f52332862eec.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2}$, we shift the graph of $y=8^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1)$, but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6351/Source/Images/73960df92124c79aec6b0d4fe0210c23.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2}-4$, we shift the graph of $y=8^{x+2}$ vertically by $4$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6351/Source/Images/05d99841f9a8ee141cf4a3e8c3648e27.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4b5807f-a10a-518a-84fa-907bf699884c" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Functions With Vertical Reflections and Horizontal Shifts"}] :question/id #uuid "6bbc31f9-c1ca-5afd-98c2-42e84545cc0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-20878" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of the function $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}$." :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6351/Source/Images/b23d927d496baadc6a16b8722b22c693.png)\n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the right to give $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6351/Source/Images/833637c510cbe4973cab4a524bea3787.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}$. The point $(4,1)$ moves to $(4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6351/Source/Images/13be27db6bf3af59c6c7c9ffb393feba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6a6d743-c559-539c-8114-4d5d07eb6b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/dcc60c2dfed55ef7d1194763c6767f8428a8b114e3b20affa01d2f68790dbde0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250720} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee0ac49-84ee-52b2-ab04-a1f1b26e13d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/12f49305204db2fde922b233f19bda2ccc3b2e34bb3ecf5ecbea7b067bc8191c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250721} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfc61de-bdc3-5c8a-a199-53df4282365b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/aeea250128938a8d1971c2ce4deceb97cb4e6190b858f7c23ab07e436e91be1c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250722} {:answer/id #uuid "6545e847-ed5a-5c5f-a1ee-4dddf24c29be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/cf21566bdb411cee489bc73ab5b6910f192da8af5613dbcf867c1d19d3e4b6df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250723} {:answer/id #uuid "be50d82e-be6a-5a49-b6cf-68aca83356e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answe